// Generated by tools/build_site.py from content/free-questions — free practice questions.
(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["cat"] = {"qs": [["Quantitative Ability", "How many integers x satisfy |2x − 5| < 9?", ["10", "8", "7", "9"], 1, "−9 < 2x − 5 < 9 gives −4 < 2x < 14, i.e. −2 < x < 7. The integers are −1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6: eight values."], ["Quantitative Ability", "A shopkeeper marks an item 40% above cost and then allows two successive discounts of 10% each. What is his profit percentage?", ["20%", "12%", "13.4%", "15.2%"], 2, "Selling price = 1.40 × 0.90 × 0.90 = 1.134 times cost, so profit is 13.4%. Subtracting 20% from 40% ignores that the second discount applies to an already reduced price."], ["Data Interpretation & Logical Reasoning", "There are 10 identical-looking coins, one of which is slightly heavier. Using only a two-pan balance, what is the minimum number of weighings that guarantees finding the heavier coin?", ["4", "5", "2", "3"], 3, "Each weighing has three outcomes, so k weighings separate at most 3^k coins. 3² = 9 < 10 ≤ 27 = 3³, so three weighings are needed and suffice."], ["Quantitative Ability", "A vessel holds 80 litres of a 30% acid solution. How many litres of pure acid must be added to raise the concentration to 50%?", ["40", "32", "16", "24"], 1, "Acid now = 24 L. Adding x L: (24 + x)/(80 + x) = 0.5, so 48 + 2x = 80 + x and x = 32. Check: 56/112 = 50%."], ["Verbal Ability & Reading Comprehension", "Read the passage and answer the question.\n\nWhen employees must actively sign up for a pension plan, participation is often modest; when they are enrolled automatically and must actively opt out, participation rises sharply, even though the choice available to them is identical. Economists once explained such gaps by the cost of filling in forms, but the paperwork involved is usually trivial. A more persuasive account is that a default carries an implicit message: people read it as the option their employer, or the experts behind the plan, consider sensible. Inertia and procrastination then do the rest. This explains why defaults are powerful, and also why they can do harm. A default contribution rate set too low may anchor savers at that level for years, leaving them worse off than if they had been made to choose for themselves.\n\nAccording to the passage, the 'cost of filling in forms' explanation is weak because:", ["employees rarely read the forms that their employers hand out at joining", "procrastination has no effect on pension decisions", "automatic enrolment removes any option of leaving the plan", "the paperwork involved is usually too small to account for the gap"], 3, "The author dismisses the form-cost explanation because 'the paperwork involved is usually trivial'. Automatic enrolment still allows opting out, and the passage says inertia and procrastination matter, so those options contradict it."], ["Data Interpretation & Logical Reasoning", "If 1 January 2025 was a Wednesday, what day of the week was 1 January 2029?", ["Monday", "Tuesday", "Saturday", "Sunday"], 0, "From 2025 to 2028 there are 3 ordinary years (1 odd day each) and the leap year 2028 (2 odd days): 5 odd days. Wednesday + 5 = Monday."], ["Data Interpretation & Logical Reasoning", "Quarterly sales (units) of four products:\nProduct | Q1 | Q2 | Q3\nA | 240 | 300 | 330\nB | 180 | 216 | 270\nC | 300 | 270 | 324\nD | 150 | 195 | 210\n\nBy approximately what percentage did the combined sales of the four products rise from Q1 to Q2?", ["11.3%", "12.8%", "10.6%", "14.2%"], 1, "Total Q1 = 240+180+300+150 = 870; total Q2 = 300+216+270+195 = 981. Increase = 111/870 × 100 ≈ 12.8%."], ["Verbal Ability & Reading Comprehension", "Four of the five sentences below can be arranged into a coherent paragraph. Identify the sentence that does NOT fit.\n\n1. Trials of a four-day working week have reported stable output alongside lower staff turnover.\n2. Critics point out that most trials involved firms that volunteered, and so were likely to succeed.\n3. Results from self-selected participants may not hold when a policy is applied across an economy.\n4. Supporters respond that even self-selected trials show the idea works in some sectors.\n5. Productivity growth in many economies slowed after the 2008 financial crisis.", ["Sentence 5", "Sentence 2", "Sentence 4", "Sentence 3"], 0, "Sentences 1-2-3-4 form a debate: trial results, a selection-bias critique, its implication, and the supporters' reply. The general post-2008 productivity slowdown does not belong to this exchange."], ["Data Interpretation & Logical Reasoning", "A shop's monthly sales (₹ lakh) for January to May were 48, 55, 42, 60 and 51. The average for January to June was ₹52 lakh. In how many of the six months were sales above the six-month average?", ["1", "3", "2", "4"], 1, "June = 6 × 52 − (48+55+42+60+51) = 312 − 256 = 56. Months above 52: February (55), April (60) and June (56), i.e. three months."], ["Data Interpretation & Logical Reasoning", "Five students scored whole-number marks (out of 100) in a test. The average is 72, the median is 75, and 80 is the only mode. What is the highest possible value of the lowest score?", ["60", "55", "62", "65"], 2, "Since 80 must appear at least twice and exceed the median, the top two scores are 80 and 80. The lowest two sum to 360 − 75 − 160 = 125 and must differ, so the best split is 62 and 63."], ["Quantitative Ability", "Find the remainder left on dividing 2²⁰²⁶ by 7.", ["1", "4", "6", "2"], 3, "2³ = 8 leaves remainder 1 on division by 7, so powers of 2 repeat in a cycle of 3 (2, 4, 1). Since 2026 = 3 × 675 + 1, 2²⁰²⁶ leaves the same remainder as 2¹, which is 2."], ["Data Interpretation & Logical Reasoning", "A committee of exactly three is to be chosen from P, Q, R, S, T and U, subject to:\n• P and Q cannot both be chosen.\n• If R is chosen, S must be chosen.\n• T can be chosen only if U is chosen.\nHow many different committees are possible?", ["12", "7", "8", "10"], 2, "Listing valid sets: PRS, PSU, PTU, QRS, QSU, QTU, RSU, STU. Any set with R but not S, T but not U, or both P and Q is excluded, leaving 8."], ["Quantitative Ability", "A train passes a 200 m platform in 20 seconds and overtakes a man walking at 6 km/h in the same direction in 12 seconds. What is the length of the train?", ["270 m", "225 m", "200 m", "250 m"], 3, "Let speed be v m/s and length L. L + 200 = 20v and L = 12(v − 5/3). So 20v − 200 = 12v − 20, giving v = 22.5 m/s and L = 250 m."], ["Verbal Ability & Reading Comprehension", "The four sentences below, when properly sequenced, form a coherent paragraph. Choose the correct order.\n\n1. This is why engineers have begun testing it as a substitute for steel reinforcement in low-cost buildings.\n2. Bamboo is technically a grass, yet some species grow nearly a metre in a single day.\n3. Its strength, too, is remarkable: weight for weight, certain species rival mild steel in tension.\n4. Such rapid growth makes it one of the most renewable building materials available.", ["4231", "2341", "2413", "2431"], 3, "Sentence 2 introduces bamboo; 4 ('Such rapid growth') follows it. Sentence 3 adds a second quality with 'too', and 1 ('This is why') draws the conclusion from both renewability and strength. Hence 2-4-3-1."], ["Data Interpretation & Logical Reasoning", "Employees of a company by department:\nDepartment | Employees | Women (%)\nHR | 80 | 45\nSales | 250 | 32\nTech | 400 | 25\nFinance | 120 | 40\nOps | 150 | 20\n\nHow many women work in the company in total?", ["294", "286", "302", "312"], 0, "Women: HR 36, Sales 80, Tech 100, Finance 48, Ops 30. Total = 294, which is 29.4% of the 1,000 employees."], ["Data Interpretation & Logical Reasoning", "Employees of a company by department:\nDepartment | Employees | Women (%)\nHR | 80 | 45\nSales | 250 | 32\nTech | 400 | 25\nFinance | 120 | 40\nOps | 150 | 20\n\nWhat is the ratio of men in Sales to men in Finance?", ["25 : 12", "17 : 6", "85 : 36", "8 : 3"], 2, "Men in Sales = 250 − 80 = 170; men in Finance = 120 − 48 = 72. Ratio 170 : 72 = 85 : 36. 25 : 12 wrongly compares total employees."], ["Verbal Ability & Reading Comprehension", "Choose the option that best captures the essence of the paragraph.\n\nWeather forecasts are often blamed when rain fails to arrive on a day marked '70 per cent chance of rain'. But such a forecast is not a promise; it means that on days with similar conditions, rain fell on about seven in ten. A forecaster whose 70 per cent days stay dry three times in ten is performing exactly as intended. Public frustration comes from reading a probability as a prediction about a single day.", ["People trusted forecasts more when they were stated in plain words.", "Weather forecasts are unreliable, since rain falls on only seventy per cent of the days predicted.", "A probabilistic forecast should be judged over many similar days, not by whether it came true on one day.", "Forecasters should drop percentages and simply say whether it will rain."], 2, "The paragraph's point is that a 70 per cent forecast is correct if it rains on about 70 per cent of such days, so it must be judged in aggregate. It does not call forecasts unreliable or propose dropping percentages."], ["Data Interpretation & Logical Reasoning", "One of four suspects A, B, C and D broke a window. Exactly one of the following statements is true.\nA: \"B did it.\"\nB: \"D did it.\"\nC: \"I did not do it.\"\nD: \"B is lying.\"\nWho broke the window?", ["C", "B", "D", "A"], 0, "B's and D's statements contradict, so exactly one of them is true. Hence A and C are both false. C's statement being false means C did it; then B is false and D is true, as required."], ["Verbal Ability & Reading Comprehension", "Choose the option that best captures the essence of the paragraph.\n\nA museum label once told visitors what an object was, where it came from and who made it. Increasingly, labels also explain how the object reached the museum: whether it was bought, gifted, or taken during war or colonial rule. Some curators see this as a distraction from the object itself; others argue that the journey is part of the object's meaning. Either way, the label has become a site of argument rather than a neutral caption.", ["By addressing how objects were acquired, museum labels have turned from neutral captions into contested statements.", "Museums should return every object that was taken during war or colonial rule.", "Visitors spend more time reading labels than looking at objects.", "Curators now agree that an object's acquisition history matters more than the object itself."], 0, "The paragraph describes labels adding acquisition history and curators disagreeing about it, so labels have become contested. Curators are said to disagree, not agree, and the paragraph makes no recommendation about returning objects."], ["Verbal Ability & Reading Comprehension", "Four of the five sentences below can be arranged into a coherent paragraph. Identify the sentence that does NOT fit.\n\n1. Fermentation was used to preserve food long before anyone understood the microbes responsible.\n2. Yoghurt, idli batter and sauerkraut all depend on bacteria that turn sugars into acids.\n3. The acidity they produce makes the food hostile to many organisms that cause spoilage.\n4. Refrigerators became common in Indian middle-class homes only in the late twentieth century.\n5. Ancient cooks thus harnessed microbiology without knowing it existed.", ["Sentence 5", "Sentence 1", "Sentence 4", "Sentence 2"], 2, "Sentences 1, 2, 3 and 5 form a chain on fermentation as unknowing use of microbes. Sentence 4 shifts to the spread of refrigerators, a different preservation story, and breaks that chain."], ["Quantitative Ability", "Given log₁₀ 2 = 0.3010, how many digits does 2⁵⁰ have?", ["17", "50", "15", "16"], 3, "log₁₀(2⁵⁰) = 50 × 0.3010 = 15.05. A number whose logarithm has integer part 15 has 15 + 1 = 16 digits."], ["Verbal Ability & Reading Comprehension", "Read the passage and answer the question.\n\nWhen employees must actively sign up for a pension plan, participation is often modest; when they are enrolled automatically and must actively opt out, participation rises sharply, even though the choice available to them is identical. Economists once explained such gaps by the cost of filling in forms, but the paperwork involved is usually trivial. A more persuasive account is that a default carries an implicit message: people read it as the option their employer, or the experts behind the plan, consider sensible. Inertia and procrastination then do the rest. This explains why defaults are powerful, and also why they can do harm. A default contribution rate set too low may anchor savers at that level for years, leaving them worse off than if they had been made to choose for themselves.\n\nThe author's attitude towards default options is best described as:", ["neutral, because the passage never evaluates defaults at all", "appreciative of their power but alert to their risks", "dismissive, since defaults merely reflect the cost of paperwork", "wholly enthusiastic about automatic enrolment in every form"], 1, "The author says the account 'explains why defaults are powerful, and also why they can do harm', citing a low default rate that anchors savers. This balanced view rules out dismissive, wholly enthusiastic or neutral readings."], ["Quantitative Ability", "A wooden cuboid measuring 12 cm × 8 cm × 6 cm is cut into identical cubes of the largest possible size with no wood wasted. How many cubes are obtained?", ["48", "72", "36", "96"], 1, "The largest cube edge is HCF(12, 8, 6) = 2 cm. Number of cubes = (12/2) × (8/2) × (6/2) = 6 × 4 × 3 = 72."], ["Data Interpretation & Logical Reasoning", "Six friends G, H, I, J, K and L sit in a row facing north in seats 1 to 6 (seat 1 is at the extreme left).\n• J and H sit at the two ends.\n• H sits immediately to the right of K.\n• Exactly two people sit between I and J.\n• L is not adjacent to I.\n• G sits somewhere to the left of K.\n\nHow many people sit between L and K?", ["1", "2", "0", "3"], 1, "The only valid arrangement from left is J, L, G, I, K, H. L is in seat 2 and K in seat 5, so G and I sit between them: two people."], ["Verbal Ability & Reading Comprehension", "The four sentences below, when properly sequenced, form a coherent paragraph. Choose the correct order.\n\n1. In experiments, pigeons fitted with small magnets lost their way when released under overcast skies.\n2. On sunny days, however, the same birds homed correctly, apparently by using the sun.\n3. Homing pigeons appear to be able to sense the Earth's magnetic field.\n4. They thus seem to carry more than one compass and use whichever is available.", ["3214", "3124", "3142", "1324"], 1, "Sentence 3 states the claim; 1 gives the magnet experiment; 2 contrasts sunny days with 'however' and 'the same birds'; 4 concludes with 'thus'. Placing 2 before 1 would leave 'the same birds' without a referent."], ["Verbal Ability & Reading Comprehension", "Read the passage and answer the question.\n\nBefore the railways, every town in Britain kept its own time, set by the sun at local noon. Bristol's clocks ran about ten minutes behind London's, and nobody minded, because few people travelled fast enough for the difference to matter. The railways changed this. A timetable listing departures in a dozen local times invited missed connections and, worse, collisions on single-track lines. Railway companies therefore adopted London time across their networks in the 1840s, and towns gradually followed, often reluctantly; some public clocks displayed two minute hands, one for local time and one for 'railway time'. Uniform time was not required by law until 1880. What looks today like a natural feature of the world was a commercial convenience that became a social habit long before it became a legal rule.\n\nWhich of the following can be inferred from the passage?", ["Most travellers before the railway era carried watches set to London time.", "Collisions on single-track lines stopped completely once London time was adopted by the railways.", "Before 1880, a British town could lawfully keep a time different from London's.", "Local time was abandoned because it was shown to be astronomically inaccurate."], 2, "Since uniform time was not required by law until 1880, keeping local time earlier was lawful. The passage never claims collisions ended entirely, and local time was in fact astronomically accurate (set by the sun)."], ["Quantitative Ability", "The sum of the first n terms of an arithmetic progression is 3n² + 5n. What is its 10th term?", ["68", "58", "66", "62"], 3, "T₁₀ = S₁₀ − S₉ = (300 + 50) − (243 + 45) = 350 − 288 = 62. In general Tₙ = 6n + 2, so the common difference is 6."], ["Quantitative Ability", "How many ordered triples of positive integers (x, y, z) satisfy x + y + z = 12?", ["55", "78", "45", "66"], 0, "By stars and bars, positive solutions of x + y + z = n number C(n − 1, 2). For n = 12 this is C(11, 2) = 55. C(12, 2) = 66 would count non-negative solutions of x + y + z = 11 instead."], ["Data Interpretation & Logical Reasoning", "Six friends G, H, I, J, K and L sit in a row facing north in seats 1 to 6 (seat 1 is at the extreme left).\n• J and H sit at the two ends.\n• H sits immediately to the right of K.\n• Exactly two people sit between I and J.\n• L is not adjacent to I.\n• G sits somewhere to the left of K.\n\nWho sits in seat 3?", ["K", "L", "I", "G"], 3, "J and H take the ends and K is immediately left of H, so H is at 6, K at 5, J at 1. I is three seats from J, at 4. L cannot be next to I, so L is at 2 and G at 3."], ["Verbal Ability & Reading Comprehension", "Read the passage and answer the question.\n\nWhen employees must actively sign up for a pension plan, participation is often modest; when they are enrolled automatically and must actively opt out, participation rises sharply, even though the choice available to them is identical. Economists once explained such gaps by the cost of filling in forms, but the paperwork involved is usually trivial. A more persuasive account is that a default carries an implicit message: people read it as the option their employer, or the experts behind the plan, consider sensible. Inertia and procrastination then do the rest. This explains why defaults are powerful, and also why they can do harm. A default contribution rate set too low may anchor savers at that level for years, leaving them worse off than if they had been made to choose for themselves.\n\nWhich of the following, if true, would most weaken the 'implicit message' explanation?", ["Participation rises just as much when employees are told the default was picked at random.", "Employees who opt out of the plan tend to earn more than those who stay enrolled in it.", "Some firms set their default contribution rates higher than other firms do.", "Many employees say they trust their employer's advice on money matters."], 0, "If a default known to be random works equally well, its power cannot come from people reading it as an expert endorsement. Trusting employer advice would strengthen the account; the other options are irrelevant to the mechanism."], ["Verbal Ability & Reading Comprehension", "Read the passage and answer the question.\n\nBefore the railways, every town in Britain kept its own time, set by the sun at local noon. Bristol's clocks ran about ten minutes behind London's, and nobody minded, because few people travelled fast enough for the difference to matter. The railways changed this. A timetable listing departures in a dozen local times invited missed connections and, worse, collisions on single-track lines. Railway companies therefore adopted London time across their networks in the 1840s, and towns gradually followed, often reluctantly; some public clocks displayed two minute hands, one for local time and one for 'railway time'. Uniform time was not required by law until 1880. What looks today like a natural feature of the world was a commercial convenience that became a social habit long before it became a legal rule.\n\nThe reference to public clocks with two minute hands mainly serves to illustrate:", ["that Bristol's local time ran ahead of London's", "a transition period when local and railway time existed side by side", "the technical superiority of railway clocks over town clocks", "the legal penalty for towns that kept local time"], 1, "Two minute hands, one for local and one for railway time, show both systems in use at once while towns 'gradually followed, often reluctantly'. Bristol actually ran behind London, so the last option misreads the passage."], ["Verbal Ability & Reading Comprehension", "Choose the option that best captures the essence of the paragraph.\n\nMuch of the world's software rests on open-source libraries maintained by a handful of unpaid volunteers. Companies that earn billions from products built on these libraries rarely contribute money or staff time to them. When a maintainer burns out or a critical flaw goes unnoticed, the damage spreads far beyond the project itself. The arrangement works only as long as goodwill holds.", ["Open-source software is less secure than commercial software because volunteers write it.", "Companies should stop using open-source libraries in products that earn revenue.", "Most software flaws are caused by maintainers who are not paid.", "Widely used open-source software rests on a thin, unpaid base of maintainers, making it more fragile than it appears."], 3, "The paragraph stresses dependence on a few unpaid maintainers and the wide damage if goodwill fails. It does not compare security with commercial software, prescribe abandoning open source, or blame most flaws on unpaid work."], ["Data Interpretation & Logical Reasoning", "On a grid of streets, Rina walks from corner (0, 0) to corner (4, 3), moving only one block east or one block north at a time. The corner (2, 1) is closed. How many different shortest routes can she take?", ["18", "17", "20", "35"], 1, "All shortest routes: C(7,3) = 35. Routes through (2,1): C(3,1) × C(4,2) = 3 × 6 = 18. Routes avoiding it = 35 − 18 = 17."], ["Verbal Ability & Reading Comprehension", "Four of the five sentences below can be arranged into a coherent paragraph. Identify the sentence that does NOT fit.\n\n1. Early chess programs relied on searching millions of positions using rules written by human experts.\n2. Newer engines instead learn by playing millions of games against themselves.\n3. Chess is believed to have originated in India as chaturanga around the sixth century.\n4. Some of their moves, once dismissed as errors, are now studied closely by grandmasters.\n5. The shift has changed not only how machines play but how humans understand the game.", ["Sentence 1", "Sentence 4", "Sentence 5", "Sentence 3"], 3, "Sentences 1, 2, 4 and 5 describe the shift from rule-based to self-taught engines and its effect on human play. The origin of chess in India is unrelated to that argument."], ["Data Interpretation & Logical Reasoning", "Five boxes V, W, X, Y and Z are stacked one above another (position 1 is the bottom).\n• X is somewhere above W.\n• Exactly one box lies between Y and X.\n• Z is directly above V.\n• W is not at the bottom.\n• Y is not at the top.\n• V is not at the bottom.\n\nWhich box is at the top?", ["W", "X", "Y", "Z"], 3, "Testing placements, the only arrangement meeting all conditions from bottom to top is Y, W, X, V, Z. Z sits directly on V at the top, X is above W, and exactly one box (W) lies between Y and X."], ["Data Interpretation & Logical Reasoning", "Quarterly sales (units) of four products:\nProduct | Q1 | Q2 | Q3\nA | 240 | 300 | 330\nB | 180 | 216 | 270\nC | 300 | 270 | 324\nD | 150 | 195 | 210\n\nFor how many products did sales increase in BOTH transitions, Q1 to Q2 and Q2 to Q3?", ["1", "2", "4", "3"], 3, "A, B and D rise in both quarters. C falls from 300 to 270 in Q2 before recovering to 324, so it does not qualify. Answer: 3."], ["Quantitative Ability", "What is the radius of the circle inscribed in a right-angled triangle whose legs are 9 cm and 12 cm?", ["3 cm", "2.5 cm", "4 cm", "6 cm"], 0, "The hypotenuse is 15 cm. For a right triangle, inradius r = (a + b − c)/2 = (9 + 12 − 15)/2 = 3 cm. Equivalently, area 54 divided by semi-perimeter 18 gives 3."], ["Quantitative Ability", "A number is picked at random from 1 to 50. What is the probability that it is divisible by 3 or by 5?", ["1/2", "21/50", "23/50", "13/25"], 2, "Multiples of 3: 16; of 5: 10; of 15: 3. By inclusion-exclusion, 16 + 10 − 3 = 23 favourable numbers, so the probability is 23/50."], ["Verbal Ability & Reading Comprehension", "The four sentences below, when properly sequenced, form a coherent paragraph. Choose the correct order.\n\n1. In them, merchants exchanged shipping news, and some became the seeds of insurance markets and stock exchanges.\n2. Coffee houses spread rapidly through London in the late seventeenth century.\n3. Unlike taverns, they offered a sober setting where strangers could talk for the price of a cup.\n4. Lloyd's of London, for example, began in a coffee house run by Edward Lloyd.", ["2341", "2134", "2314", "3214"], 2, "Sentence 2 opens the topic; 3 describes what coffee houses offered ('they'); 1 ('In them') explains their commercial role; 4 gives Lloyd's as an example of an insurance market born there. Hence 2-3-1-4."], ["Quantitative Ability", "Two runners start together from the same point on a circular track and run in opposite directions at 5 m/s and 7 m/s. At how many distinct points on the track will they meet?", ["12", "35", "7", "2"], 0, "For runners moving in opposite directions with speeds in the lowest ratio a : b, the number of distinct meeting points is a + b = 5 + 7 = 12. Running in the same direction would give |a − b| = 2."], ["Quantitative Ability", "A and B together can finish a job in 10 days, B and C in 15 days, and A and C in 12 days. How long will A, B and C take working together?", ["6 days", "7.5 days", "8 days", "9 days"], 2, "Adding pair rates: 2(A + B + C) = 1/10 + 1/15 + 1/12 = 15/60 = 1/4. So A + B + C = 1/8 per day, i.e. 8 days together."], ["Verbal Ability & Reading Comprehension", "Read the passage and answer the question.\n\nBefore the railways, every town in Britain kept its own time, set by the sun at local noon. Bristol's clocks ran about ten minutes behind London's, and nobody minded, because few people travelled fast enough for the difference to matter. The railways changed this. A timetable listing departures in a dozen local times invited missed connections and, worse, collisions on single-track lines. Railway companies therefore adopted London time across their networks in the 1840s, and towns gradually followed, often reluctantly; some public clocks displayed two minute hands, one for local time and one for 'railway time'. Uniform time was not required by law until 1880. What looks today like a natural feature of the world was a commercial convenience that became a social habit long before it became a legal rule.\n\nThe primary purpose of the passage is to:", ["show that standard time began as a practical arrangement before it acquired legal force", "argue that the railways caused most of the collisions recorded in nineteenth-century Britain", "explain why Bristol resisted London time longer than other towns", "describe how clocks with two minute hands were engineered"], 0, "The passage traces uniform time from a railway convenience to a social habit and only later to law (1880). Collisions, Bristol and the dual-hand clocks are supporting details, not the main purpose."], ["Quantitative Ability", "A sum of money lent at compound interest (compounded annually) amounts to ₹13,310 in 3 years and ₹14,641 in 4 years. What is the sum?", ["₹11,000", "₹10,500", "₹10,000", "₹9,500"], 2, "Ratio of successive amounts 14,641/13,310 = 1.1, so the rate is 10%. Principal = 13,310 / 1.1³ = 13,310 / 1.331 = ₹10,000."], ["Quantitative Ability", "If f(x) = 2x + 3 and g(x) = x² − 1, what is f(g(2)) − g(f(1))?", ["−15", "−21", "15", "−9"], 0, "g(2) = 3, so f(g(2)) = 9. f(1) = 5, so g(f(1)) = 24. The difference is 9 − 24 = −15; reversing the order of composition gives the tempting 15."], ["Data Interpretation & Logical Reasoning", "Quarterly sales (units) of four products:\nProduct | Q1 | Q2 | Q3\nA | 240 | 300 | 330\nB | 180 | 216 | 270\nC | 300 | 270 | 324\nD | 150 | 195 | 210\n\nWhich product recorded the highest percentage growth in sales from Q1 to Q3?", ["C", "B", "D", "A"], 1, "Growth Q1 to Q3: A 90/240 = 37.5%, B 90/180 = 50%, C 24/300 = 8%, D 60/150 = 40%. B leads even though A has the same absolute increase of 90 units."]]};
